<!-- cell: title-page id="7ab3f1b3-c8af-4028-845f-c9b1b4653cbf" title="Hostage" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="9372c0ae-4ff7-4b44-8899-7ccf9668b62d" -->

Eleven thousand Swarm ships hung above the gas giant Oro like frost on a window, and the flagship Meridian was the only human vessel close enough to see them with the naked eye.

On the bridge, Captain Ilse Varn kept her hands clasped behind her back so that her crew would not see them tremble. Behind her, the Oro moons held nine million people, her daughter among them, and no fleet worth the name.

"Their envoy is docking," said First Officer Dace. "We have every torpedo locked onto that shuttle, Captain. One word." "No," said Varn.

The envoy Th'raan came aboard in a column of cold mist, tall and many-jointed, its voice arriving a second late through the translator, and it spoke on the open bridge, in front of everyone.

"Three of your ships fired on our nursery fleet at Kesh," it said. "Seven hundred of our young did not wake. You say it was a mistake. We say a mistake is a word. We require a guarantee."

"What guarantee would satisfy you?" asked Varn. "None that your kind has ever offered."

Dace leaned close. "Let me end this, Captain. While it's standing here."

Varn thought of her daughter, who believed her mother could fix anything, and understood what fixing this would cost.

"Then I offer something new," she said. "Me. I will go with you as your hostage, for as long as the peace holds. If a human ship fires on yours again, you may do with me what you like."

Th'raan's many eyes closed and opened, slowly, one pair after another. "You would leave your ship," it said. "I would leave everything."

"Captain," said Dace, "I won't allow it."

Varn took the command key from around her neck and pressed it into his hand. "You're relieved, Commander. You have the Meridian. Keep the guns cold."

He stared at the key for a long moment. Then he saluted, and his voice did not break as he ordered the weapons stood down.

Varn walked into the mist beside the envoy, and on every screen of the Meridian the frost above Oro began to thin as ship after ship turned away into the dark.

Three years later the peace still held, and every evening, in a garden built for her on a Swarm world, Ilse Varn wrote a letter to a daughter who was no longer a child.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="03971a07-384c-493d-b119-0a2887dcd959" title="Captain Ilse Varn" words="33-73, 93-95, 168-174, 194-212, 213-251, 270-273, 281-306, 332-362, 363-396" -->

Commander of the flagship Meridian, frightened and hiding it. Above all she wants to keep nine million colonists — her daughter among them — alive, and she is willing to give herself up to do it.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="2cf0e9c6-e41c-429e-a2d4-8b98547f037e" title="Th'raan" words="96-129, 130-167, 175-181, 252-269" -->

The many-jointed, many-eyed envoy of the Swarm, grieving seven hundred lost young. It trusts no human promise and demands a guarantee no human has ever given.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="9ac57fd6-91d4-4e59-a647-335f9f9b462f" title="The flagship Meridian above Oro" words="0-32, 33-73, 96-129, 281-306, 332-362" -->

The only human ship facing eleven thousand Swarm ships above the gas giant Oro: its open bridge, where the fate of the Oro moons is decided in front of the crew.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="c23b2ba4-63d1-4ae9-a018-c26823bb20e6" title="War" words="0-32, 74-92, 130-167, 182-193, 274-280, 307-331, 332-362" -->

The war about to begin after human ships destroy a Swarm nursery fleet: the eleven thousand ships, the torpedoes already locked, the first officer's itch to fire.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="8381a694-6439-48b8-9154-8b2abe3642aa" title="Sacrifice" words="194-212, 213-251, 263-273, 281-306, 363-396" -->

Varn gives up her ship, her command and her life with her daughter so that the peace will hold.

<!-- cell: plot id="72b02030-776a-436c-bed2-af3b2cace7a4" title="The hostage" nodes="n1:0-32, n2:96-129, n3:130-167, n4:213-251, n5:263-273, n6:332-362, n7:363-396" -->

The Swarm demands a guarantee for the killing at Kesh; Varn offers herself as hostage, the envoy accepts, and the Swarm fleet withdraws; years later the peace still holds.

<!-- cell: plot id="27f84e4c-7256-4e11-a3ef-cd37683ba439" title="Dace and the guns" nodes="n1:74-95, n2:182-193, n3:274-280, n4:281-306, n5:307-331" -->

Her first officer wants to fire on the envoy and refuses to let her go; Varn relieves him and hands him the ship, and he chooses to stand the weapons down.
